  "generated_text": "1. We start with the given line equation \\( y = \\frac{1}{2} x + 4 \\).\n2. We need to find the parameterized form \\( \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ s \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} l \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n3. From the parameterized form, we know that \\( x = -7 + lt \\) and \\( y = s - 5t \\).\n4. Substitute \\( x = -7 + lt \\) into the line equation:\n   \\[\n   s - 5t = \\frac{1}{2}(-7 + lt) + 4\n   \\]\n5. Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   s - 5t = \\frac{1}{2}(-7 + lt) + 4\n   \\]\n6. By comparing the coefficients of \\( t \\) on both sides of the equation, we find that \\( s = 1 \\) and \\( l = 2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the ordered pair \\((s, l)\\) is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{(1, 2)}\\)",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[Interval.open(1, 2), '(1, 2)']",
    "gold_answer": "Taking $t = 0,$ we find $\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ s \\end{pmatrix}$ lies on the line.  Then\n\\[s = \\frac{1}{2} (-7) + 4 = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]Taking $t = 1,$ we get\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 1/2 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} l \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 + l \\\\ -9/2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Then\n\\[-\\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} (-7 + l) + 4.\\]Solving for $l,$ we find $l = -10.$\n\nHence, $(r,k) = \\boxed{\\left( \\frac{1}{2}, -10 \\right)}.$",
    "is_correct": false
  },
